  "solution": "<p>Completing the squares gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow></math>, so the original centre is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A radius is perpendicular to its tangent, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02220;</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02220;</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>90</mn><mo>&#x02218;</mo></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>The circle with diameter OP therefore passes through both A and B, by the right-angle-in-a-semicircle result. It also passes through P, so it is the requested circle through P, A and B.</p><p>The diameter has length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>80</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. Its radius is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msqrt><mrow><mn>80</mn></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>. Answer B.</p>",
